01. IAM Role for EC2

This is much more important for DevOps than storing AWS credentials on the server.

Bad approach:

EC2
 └── AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
 └── AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY

Better:

EC2
 ↓
IAM Role
 ↓
Temporary credentials
 ↓
AWS APIs

Example:

EC2 needs to download artifacts from S3:

EC2
 ↓
IAM Role
 ↓
S3 permission
 ↓
aws s3 cp ...

No hardcoded access keys.



02. User Data:
User Data allows you to execute commands during instance initialization.

Example:

#!/bin/bash

dnf update -y
dnf install -y nginx
systemctl enable nginx
systemctl start nginx

When EC2 starts:

EC2 Launch
   ↓
User Data
   ↓
Install software
   ↓
Configure server
   ↓
Application ready

This is extremely useful for automation.



03. Real DevOps Example — EC2 Bootstrap
Imagine you need 10 servers.

Manual:

Server 1 → SSH → Install Docker
Server 2 → SSH → Install Docker
Server 3 → SSH → Install Docker
...
Server 10

Bad DevOps approach.

Instead:

Launch Template
      ↓
User Data
      ↓
Install Docker
      ↓
Configure application
      ↓
Ready

Then Auto Scaling can launch identical instances.



04. ENI
ENI = Elastic Network Interface

Think of it as the network interface attached to EC2.

It has things such as:

Private IP
Security Groups
MAC address
Subnet association

Conceptually:

EC2
 |
 ENI
 |
Private IP
 |
Subnet

This becomes especially useful when troubleshooting networking.



05. Elastic IP
An Elastic IP is a static public IPv4 address associated with your AWS account that you can associate with an EC2 instance or other supported resources.

Why?

Normal public IP:

Stop EC2
 ↓
Start EC2
 ↓
Public IP may change

Elastic IP:

Static public IPv4

However, in modern production architectures, you often avoid putting an Elastic IP directly on application servers.

Instead:

Internet
 ↓
ALB
 ↓
EC2
